All seven fixes are in and committed locally as 8348de1. I did not push. `npm test` (410 tests, up from 346), `npx tsc --noEmit`, `npm run build` and `npm run web:build` pass.

I recorded "Post-review fixes 3" in both `tasks.md` files before coding, and filled in the results afterwards. The measurements below come from fresh clones of click, black and koa, plus the 6 research repos.

1. **Latency.** Each line is now normalized and each k-gram hashed once. Only windows that can still reach warn get fingerprinted, so results don't change. Properties compare the new windowing with `matchWindowed`, including comment openers and closers.

   | Case | Before | After |
   |---|---|---|
   | click `core.py` (4,019 lines) | 4.3 s | 45 ms |
   | black `test_black.py` | 3.0 s | 280 ms |
   | slowest of black's 529 files in `check` | 36 s | 357 ms |

   The new benchmark test (4,000 lines, 1,500 tombstones, with and without block comments) takes about 300 ms against a 500 ms budget. A 332,000-line file takes 5.4 s, which I did not try to improve.
2. **Comment fragments.** Comment-only deleted blocks are dropped. The diff's context lines also tell when a hunk starts inside a `/* */`. The koa JSDoc probe now exits 0.
3. **`minHashes`.** The default is 4, set with `--min-hashes` in the miner and `minHashes` in `config.json` for the watcher. It drops 196 of 6,166 tombstones (3.2%) on the 6 research repos. The three reviewer probes now exit 0. Table is in `research/matching-eval.md` section 8.
4. **HEAD as `before`.** `check` and MCP `check_snippet` (when given a `path`) compare with HEAD's version of the file. Alarms on unchanged HEAD files went from 18 to 0 on click, 31 to 0 on koa and 22 to 0 on black. `check --no-head` keeps the old behaviour.
5. **Documented only.** The README has a "What it can't see" section with the numbers. I did not merge same-commit hunks, because I had no false-alarm measurement.
6. **New-user path.** `index` now writes `<repo>/.deja-boo/graveyard.json` by default. The README has "Use it on your repo". With no graveyard, the hook prints one stderr line and exits 0, and the Stop hook prints the same line. I checked the flow on a fresh clone.
7. **Lock files.** All the listed patterns are skipped by default.

**`minHashes` costs 2 of the 12 clearly real comebacks.** The dropped tombstones carried 6 revivals (50 to 44, none via Revert). Two of those tombstones were in your hand-labelled set as real: express `lib/utils.js` e66667e4 and flask `src/flask/cli.py` 4f03a769. The README and research say so, and `--min-hashes 1` plus `"minHashes": 1` brings them back.

I re-ran `npm run web:data` because the counts changed. The 6 repos now total 5,970 tombstones (was 6,301). Commits went from 19,908 to 19,909 because axios has one new upstream commit. I updated the README and research numbers.

Replaying the first 8 to 12 revert commits of each repo (31 total) gives identical results before and after: 2, 0 and 0 caught on click, black and koa. No detection was lost.

- **Changed test expectations:** the "exit 1 for a missing graveyard" test now expects exit 0 with one line. The `findRevenants` prefilter property now passes `minHashes: 0` so it keeps testing what it tested before.
